WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.900
- [Jon Sindreu] This is Boeing's 737,

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.430
and this is the Airbus A320.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.090
They're by far the most
popular passenger planes

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.200
sold around the world

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.670
with over a fifth of them flying in China,

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.400
making it a key market.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.060
- This is a continuation
of a long-term strategy

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.620
of Airbus in China.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.760
- But these two rivals

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.830
may soon have a new direct competitor.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.710
The C919 made by Chinese state-owned

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.420
aviation company Comac.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.010
These planes are expected to enter

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.840
into service later this year

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.550
and state-owned airlines in China

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.960
have already ordered or expressed interest

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.870
in more than 1000 of them.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.930
Comac is owned and heavily
subsidized by Beijing.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.610
And that makes the company potentially

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.790
the biggest challenge to a duopoly

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.120
that has dominated the market
for over three decades.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.510
- I'm looking forward to...

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.500
- [Jon Sindreu] The rise of
China's aviation industry

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.400
is one big reason why the
US and the European union

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.270
have called a truce on
a 17 year tariff fight

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.700
over government subsidies.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.170
- Others are taking the opportunity

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.060
to launch their own industries.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.580
And we have been too busy,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.020
fighting each other to pay attention.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.800
- So how can Airbus and
Boeing counter Comac.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.840
Technology, their global reach

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.510
and growing political support

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.083
could be some of the answers.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.570
Boeing and Airbus have been
rivals since the 1960s.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.910
Back then, the European company was seen

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.860
as an overly subsidized laggard,

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.020
but after 1988, Airbus' A320

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.400
allowed it to knock Boeing off
the top spot several times.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.240
So facing new competition,
Boeing nudged the US

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.540
to start a legal fight in 2004,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.500
and then...

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.630
- The EU has today decided
to take the United States

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.430
to the World Trade Organization

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:43.640
over the massive subsidies
they gave to Boeing.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.220
- [Jon Sindreu] It became the
longest and most costly battle

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.400
in the history of the
World Trade Organization.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.603
Resulting in more than a
billion dollars in tariffs.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.750
Lately, Airbus has pulled ahead of Boeing

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:55.770
in terms of orders.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.050
That's also because Boeing
has been dealing with a major

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.730
crisis since the grounding
of its 737 Max planes,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.630
After a string of crashes.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.370
- All of us feel the immense
gravity of these events

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:07.550
across our company.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.380
- The Corona virus pandemic has
also hit both companies hard

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.030
with both delivering
hundreds fewer planes,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.220
but there's one market that
wasn't doing us badly, China.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.720
At one point, China became

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.290
the world's largest aviation market.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.093
As the US canceled flights.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.720
- [Jon Sindreu] Now the US and Europe

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.010
have decided to suspend

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.280
their trade fight to work
together and focus on China.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.420
- To confront the threat
from China's ambitions

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.960
to build an aircraft sector...

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.270
- [Jon Sindreu] Airbus told us
that Comac could take a share

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.040
of the single aisle market
by the end of the decade.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.970
And in China, that direct competition

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.050
is about to start.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.967
(foreign language)

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.400
- [Jon Sindreu] Since 2008,
Comac alone has received

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.450
between 49 and $72
billion in government aid,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.270
according to industry watchers.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:00.740
and has successfully built
a regional plane, the ARJ21.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.530
The C919 has cost between 9 to $20 billion

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.363
to develop.

00:03:05.363 --> 00:03:06.890
It's designed to directly compete

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:07.970
with narrow-body planes

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.320
like the 737 and the A320,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.230
which can handle a range of
short and medium haul routes.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.680
The Chinese aircraft
is less fuel efficient,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.800
has a shorter range and lacks
the global service network

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.610
of the Western plane makers.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.810
But China alone will
need some 8,600 planes

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.440
over the next 20 years,
according to Boeing.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.330
- I believe their need for
aircraft is significant.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.370
- And Comac wants to
provide many of those.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.840
Orders for Western planes
from Chinese airlines

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.450
are much lower than what
demand would suggest

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.483
indicating that they may
plan to buy the C919 instead.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.660
China and Russia are now
also developing the CR929.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.970
A long haul wide-body aircraft

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.276
that should start being built this year.

00:03:54.276 --> 00:03:55.109
(tech music)

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:57.840
So how could Airbus and
Boeing counter Comac?

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.580
Well, first there is manufacturing.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.340
Boeing has been developing
planes for over a century

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.140
and relies on decades of experience

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.490
and intellectual property

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.740
to deploy the latest plane technology.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.680
Comac is some 90 years
younger than Boeing.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.920
And it's C919 has already
faced production delays.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.900
It will initially only be able to produce

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.870
very few C919s each month.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.580
Boeing and Airbus have
also used their duopoly

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.290
to create an international
network of parts, servicing

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.900
and mechanics to ensure their planes

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.570
can operate around the globe.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.180
Even if Comac's planes
become successful in China,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.510
they might be hard to export.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.330
And then there is concessions.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:39.010
Airbus has already
received around $13 billion

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.550
in pandemic bailout support,

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:41.990
which it is partly investing

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.870
into researching hydrogen planes.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.970
- One particular technology pathway,

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:49.960
which we find most promising, is hydrogen.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.490
- Boeing and Airbus's new found truce

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.870
could help them pose
legal challenges to Comac

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:55.750
while still trying to extract concessions

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.310
from their own governments
to develop new, cheaper

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.420
and more fuel-efficient planes.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.810
Finally, there is the fact

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.820
that the C919 is built mostly on parts

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.920
made by Western suppliers.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.570
Engines are the hardest
part of the plane to build.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.150
And the C919 might still be a decade away

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.130
from flying on Chinese-made propulsion.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.700
For now, it uses engines

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.890
made by General Electric and Safran.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.460
The US and EU don't want
to hurt these companies,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.980
but geopolitical tensions could
eventually reach them too.

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.010
China's already caught up with
the west in many industries

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.760
like trains, cars, and digital technology.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.780
So the C919 may be a stepping stone

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.450
to posing a real challenge
in aerospace as well.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.680
After all the country
can potentially instruct

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.310
it's state-owned airlines to buy

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.606
as many homemade planes as it desires.

00:05:41.606 --> 00:05:44.856
(tech music continues)

